{#
  The Traefik dashboard's door. It existed at the Docker edge as a file-provider router
  named `dashboard` in roles/containers/traefik/templates/config.yml.j2, and went away with
  that host in e9a9569c ("E7 slice 3: the Docker edge stands down") — the migration ported
  every app route but not Traefik's own, so `traefik.local.<domain>` has 404'd since.
  `api.dashboard: true` stayed set the whole time (static-config.yaml.j2:10), so the
  dashboard was running with nothing routed to it.

  NOT the shared ingressroute() macro, and it cannot be: that macro emits
  `services[].name` + `port`, which resolves to a k8s Service. The dashboard is served by
  Traefik's own internal service, which is reached by NAME with `kind: TraefikService` and
  NO port. Give it the default kind and Traefik looks for a Service called `api@internal`
  in this namespace, finds nothing, and the route 404s exactly as if it were absent.
  Neither pre-deploy gate can catch that mistake: IngressRoute is a CRD so prek only proves
  the YAML parses, and `traefik` is in k8s_dry_run_unsupported so --dry-run refuses the role
  outright. It is verified live or not at all.
#}
apiVersion: traefik.io/v1alpha1
kind: IngressRoute
metadata:
  name: traefik-dashboard
  namespace: {{ k8s_namespace }}
spec:
  entryPoints:
    - https
  routes:
    - kind: Rule
      # DECIDED: LAN-only. The retired Docker router also matched the public
      # `traefik.<domain>`, and that host is deliberately NOT reproduced here — the
      # dashboard enumerates every router, service and middleware on this edge, which is a
      # map of the whole homelab, and Authelia in front of it does not make publishing that
      # map worthwhile. This route ignores k8s_public_route for the same reason
      # prometheus-monitoring does: `public=false` is the convention for infrastructure APIs
      # (see ansible/templates/ingressroute.yml.j2's macro header). WireGuard peers still
      # reach it — they resolve `.local.` names through Pi-hole — because the gate here is
      # the hostname, not a ClientIP matcher.
      #
      # Host-only, no PathPrefix, matching the retired router. The documented upstream form
      # adds (PathPrefix(`/dashboard`) || PathPrefix(`/api`)), which leaves a bare
      # `https://traefik.local.<domain>/` at 404 — the exact symptom that sent someone
      # looking for this route in the first place.
      match: "Host(`traefik.local.{{ domain }}`)"
      middlewares:
        # rate-limit is per-router on this edge, not on the https entrypoint. default-headers
        # is NOT listed: it is already global on the entrypoint (static-config.yaml.j2), so
        # repeating it here would be a no-op — the same note the Docker router carried.
        - name: rate-limit
        - name: authelia
      services:
        # kind + absent port are both load-bearing. See the header.
        - name: api@internal
          kind: TraefikService
  # Not boilerplate. An IngressRoute on the https entrypoint with no tls block becomes a
  # NON-TLS router and is never a candidate for an HTTPS request — it does not lose a
  # priority contest, it is not in the running at all. ENFORCED by
  # ansible/tests/k8s/test_k8s_manifests.py. Do not shorten to the file provider's `tls: {}`;
  # that shorthand is not IngressRoute syntax.
  tls:
{% if k8s_tls_cert_resolver %}
    certResolver: {{ k8s_tls_cert_resolver }}
{% endif %}
    options:
      name: modern
{% if k8s_tls_cert_resolver %}
    domains:
      - main: "{{ domain }}"
        sans:
          - "*.{{ domain }}"
          - "*.local.{{ domain }}"
{% endif %}
